{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778869959,"nonce":"0e0i1f/8QI0kitvIVGurEOkIqWg=","message":"Mandible should be able to coordinate specialized research tools without absorbing their internals. Feedbax is the immediate case, but the design should handle other providers later.\n\nMandible should provide:\n\n- Provider installation/health/capability discovery.\n- Stable artifact storage and metadata indexing.\n- Manifest ingestion and graph/provenance indexing.\n- Review/auth policy for provider actions.\n- Agent-facing provider schema/action discovery.\n- UI action placement on compatible graph nodes.\n\nProviders should provide:\n\n- Domain schemas.\n- Domain validation.\n- Domain execution.\n- Domain-specific UI entry points.\n- Domain-specific registries and extension contracts.\n- Domain manifests that Mandible can ingest without interpreting every field.\n\n\nMandible currently has no real provider/artifact substrate. The word `provider` appears mainly for AI provider session IDs and conversation search, not domain-tool providers. There is also no artifact table, no content-addressed local artifact store, no manifest ingest API, and no provider action registry.\n\nIssue `2472c51` already captures the right high-level direction: Mandible should own project/provenance graph, artifact store, plugin registry, agent runtime, and review/auth policy, while tools such as Feedbax expose schemas, validation, execution, component registries, UI entry points, and emitted manifests/artifacts.\n\nThis spec makes that direction more concrete.\n\n\nA provider registry entry should include:\n\n- Stable provider ID, such as `feedbax`.\n- Display name and description.\n- Installed version and provider contract version.\n- Source repo/package identity.\n- Health check command or endpoint.\n- Capability manifest retrieval command or endpoint.\n- Supported transports: CLI, HTTP, Python, MCP-style, or static manifest.\n- Supported artifact roles and media types.\n- Supported manifest kinds and schema versions.\n- UI entry points, such as open in provider studio.\n- Trust/review policy defaults.\n- Last health status and diagnostics.\n\nIllustrative table:\n\n```sql\nproviders (\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  contract_version TEXT NOT NULL,\n  installed_version TEXT,\n  source_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  manifest_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  transport_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  health_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'unknown',\n  created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  updated_at INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  last_checked_at INTEGER\n);\n```\n\nProvider registration can start as local config plus a CLI refresh command. Self-registration over HTTP or MCP can come later. The important property is that Mandible can ask, cache, and display what providers are installed and healthy.\n\n\nMandible should store provider capability manifests mostly as opaque provider-owned JSON, but it should normalize enough fields to drive graph actions and validation.\n\nA provider capability should include:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"feedbax.validate_training_spec\",\n  \"label\": \"Validate training spec\",\n  \"kind\": \"validate\",\n  \"input_schema_ref\": \"feedbax.TrainingRunSpec/v1\",\n  \"output_schema_ref\": \"feedbax.ValidationResult/v1\",\n  \"compatible_entities\": [\"provider_spec\", \"issue\", \"artifact\"],\n  \"compatible_artifact_roles\": [\"feedbax_training_spec\"],\n  \"transport\": {\"kind\": \"cli\", \"command\": \"feedbax provider validate\"},\n  \"requires_review\": false,\n  \"mutates_state\": false,\n  \"may_launch_compute\": false\n}\n```\n\nCapability kinds should probably include:\n\n- `open`: open a provider UI for a compatible node.\n- `preview`: produce a summary/preview for an artifact or manifest.\n- `validate`: validate a provider spec.\n- `import`: import external/local data into provider/Mandible manifests.\n- `execute`: run training/evaluation/analysis/report generation.\n- `publish`: publish provider outputs back into Mandible.\n- `register_extension`: register provider-specific component/analysis/task/loss/etc. extensions.\n- `migrate`: migrate older manifests/artifacts/schema versions.\n\nMandible does not need to know the domain semantics behind `feedbax.TrainingRunSpec`; it needs to know that the capability is a validation action, which entity/artifact types it applies to, what transport to invoke, whether it mutates state, and whether review/auth is required.\n\n\nProvider actions should be generated from capability manifests and selected graph node context. The action registry should answer:\n\n- What actions are available for this selected node or edge?\n- Are their providers installed and healthy?\n- Does this node's entity/artifact/manifest type satisfy the capability predicate?\n- Does the action require review/auth?\n- Does it mutate local state, write artifacts, launch compute, or change provider registries?\n- Where should results be attached in the graph?\n\nAction descriptors returned to the UI should include:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"feedbax.open_studio\",\n  \"provider_id\": \"feedbax\",\n  \"label\": \"Open in Feedbax Studio\",\n  \"icon\": \"external-link\",\n  \"enabled\": true,\n  \"disabled_reason\": null,\n  \"requires_review\": false,\n  \"confirmation\": null,\n  \"target_entity_id\": \"provider_spec:...\"\n}\n```\n\nThis should replace hardcoded UI conditions like `if provider === feedbax`. Provider-specific UI components may still exist for rich detail panes, but action visibility and basic invocation should be data-driven.\n\n\nMandible should provide a local content-addressed artifact store first. A minimal local layout could be:\n\n```text\n~/.cache/mandible/artifacts/\n  sha256/\n    ab/\n      abcdef...          # content blob or directory bundle\n  manifests/\n  previews/\n```\n\nArtifact records should include:\n\n- Stable artifact ID, preferably `artifact://sha256/\u003chash\u003e` for immutable content.\n- Hash algorithm and hash value.\n- Logical name.\n- Role, such as `training_checkpoint`, `trajectory_dataset`, `analysis_table`, `figure`, `report`, `provider_manifest`, `run_log`.\n- Media type.\n- Size in bytes.\n- Storage backend and URI/path.\n- Provider ID and producer entity/manifest ID.\n- Repo/project association.\n- Created/ingested timestamps.\n- Metadata JSON.\n- Retention/pin policy.\n- Integrity check status.\n\nIllustrative table:\n\n```sql\nartifacts (\n  id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n  sha256 TEXT,\n  logical_name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  role TEXT NOT NULL,\n  media_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n  size_bytes INTEGER,\n  storage_backend TEXT NOT NULL,\n  uri TEXT,\n  provider_id TEXT,\n  producer_entity_id TEXT,\n  repo_id TEXT,\n  metadata_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  retention_json TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',\n  created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  ingested_at INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  integrity_status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'unchecked'\n);\n```\n\nFor directory artifacts, Mandible should either package them deterministically or record a manifest of file paths and hashes. Avoid pretending a mutable directory path is a stable artifact ID.\n\n\nProvider manifests should be first-class graph inputs. Mandible should accept a manifest object or manifest artifact reference and create/refresh graph entities and edges from normalized sections.\n\nA generic manifest ingest API might accept:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"provider_id\": \"feedbax\",\n  \"manifest_kind\": \"TrainingRunManifest\",\n  \"schema_version\": \"feedbax.manifest.v1\",\n  \"manifest\": {},\n  \"artifact_refs\": [],\n  \"source\": {\n    \"repo_id\": \"rlrmp\",\n    \"issue_ids\": [\"...\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nMandible should normalize:\n\n- Provider manifest identity.\n- Parent/child manifest edges.\n- Artifact references.\n- Source repo/commit/dirty status if provided.\n- Issue/session/auth/work-unit provenance if provided.\n- Summary metrics and status fields useful for graph labels and filtering.\n- Provider-specific detail JSON stored opaquely for later provider rendering.\n\nMandible should not attempt to validate all provider-domain fields. It can require that provider ID, manifest kind, schema version, identity, artifact references, and provenance edge declarations satisfy the provider contract. Domain validation belongs to the provider.\n\n\nProvider ingest should populate an exhaustive provenance graph automatically. Example edges:\n\n- issue planned provider spec.\n- session authored provider spec.\n- auth request approved execution.\n- provider run used spec.\n- provider run emitted manifest.\n- manifest references artifact.\n- analysis used training run.\n- report used analysis manifest.\n- artifact derived from artifact.\n- commit added spec.\n- commit resolved issue.\n\nThis graph can be noisy and should be filterable. It should not be confused with curated project semantics.\n\n\nMandible should also support curated semantic nodes and edges. These may be manually created or agent-proposed. They are not merely observed facts; they are claims or organizational structure.\n\nExamples:\n\n- issue A `motivates` issue B.\n- analysis result `supports` training-method decision.\n- artifact/report `is_evidence_for` claim.\n- provider spec `tests` hypothesis.\n- result `contradicts` prior finding.\n- issue `supersedes` older issue.\n\nCurated edges need provenance of assertion: who/what asserted it, when, source text, confidence/review state, and whether it was human-approved. This is where Mandible may later overlap with HumbleB; the graph substrate should not block shared graph rendering or richer epistemic metadata.\n\n\nProvider actions must be classified by risk:\n\n- Pure read/open/preview actions: no auth; maybe health check only.\n- Validation actions: no auth unless they execute untrusted code.\n- Import/ingest actions: may require confirmation because they mutate Mandible indexes/artifact store.\n- Local execution actions: review required if expensive, long-running, or writing artifacts.\n- Registry-extension actions: review required if they add executable code, provider plugins, or local component registries.\n- Destructive artifact actions: auth required.\n- Remote compute or credentialed actions: auth/review required according to provider policy.\n\nMandible's existing auth request generalized type field is relevant here. Provider actions that need approval should create auth/action requests with `type` values beyond `merge`, carrying provider ID, capability ID, target entity, artifact/spec refs, and execution mode.\n\n\nAgents should be able to ask Mandible:\n\n- List installed providers and health.\n- List capabilities for a node/artifact/spec.\n- Fetch provider schemas or schema references.\n- Validate a spec with the provider before asking for execution.\n- Ingest an artifact/manifest and receive stable Mandible IDs.\n- Propose curated graph edges with source text.\n- Submit reviewed provider actions through the auth queue.\n\nThe goal is bounded autonomy. Agents should not guess how to open Feedbax Studio, where artifacts should live, or which graph nodes to create. They should call Mandible/provider contracts and record provenance.\n\n\nFor Feedbax, Mandible should be able to represent:\n\n- `feedbax` provider node.\n- Feedbax project/repo nodes.\n- Graph/model/task/loss/training/evaluation/analysis/report specs as provider_spec nodes.\n- Training/evaluation/analysis/report manifests as provider_manifest or run/report nodes.\n- Checkpoints, histories, trajectory datasets, analysis tables, figures, and reports as artifact nodes.\n- Edges from issues/sessions/commits/auth requests to Feedbax specs and runs.\n- Actions such as open in Feedbax Studio, validate graph spec, validate training spec, start training run, run analysis, publish report, preview artifact.\n\nMandible should not know Feedbax component registry semantics internally. It should know enough to store Feedbax's registry snapshot as provider data and route validation/action calls back to Feedbax.\n\n\nA practical first slice:\n\n1. Add provider registry schema/config and a static manifest loader.\n2. Add provider health/capabilities API.\n3. Add local content-addressed artifact ingest API.\n4. Add artifact records and graph entity creation for artifacts.\n5. Add provider manifest ingest API.\n6. Normalize provider manifests into graph entities/edges.\n7. Add node action descriptors for provider open/validate/preview actions.\n8. Wire one provider in minimal form, probably Feedbax open/validate/ingest before execution.\n9. Add graph mode `provider-lineage` once artifacts/manifests exist.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}